//  PETPORTS -- HELP TOOLTIP CARRIER
//
//  AN ITEM THAT EXISTS TO BE A TOOLTIP. It is never crafted, never dropped,
//  never held and never enters an inventory: the only thing that ever puts it
//  anywhere is widget.setItemSlotItem, into the `helpSlot` of a settings row.
//
//  ---- WHY AN ITEM AT ALL ------------------------------------------------
//
//  fact.pane.notooltips: a ContainerPane does not forward createTooltip to its
//  script, so this pane hand-draws its hover text on two canvases
//  (arch.pane.hoverlayer). That layer reads a widget's rect with
//  widget.getPosition and matches the cursor against it, which works for every
//  widget declared at pane level and CANNOT work for a widget inside a list
//  row: a row member's position is relative to a list that scrolls, and
//  nothing exposes a scrollArea's offset.
//
//  The one kind of tooltip a ContainerPane still produces is the one
//  ItemSlotWidget draws for itself, from the item it is holding. So a row that
//  wants help holds an item, and the item carries the words.
//
//  ---- WHY THE WORDS ARE NOT IN THIS FILE --------------------------------
//
//  They are per row -- twenty-odd of them -- and they already live in
//  petports_strings.config under petport.tip.*, beside every other string in
//  the mod (arch.pane.stringtable). The row builder resolves its own key and
//  passes `shortdescription` and `description` as DESCRIPTOR PARAMETERS.
//
//  That works because of fact.item.instanceicon read forwards: the engine
//  merges parameters over config at INSTANTIATION, where instanceValue checks
//  parameters and falls back to the asset. The slot instantiates, so the
//  overrides are what the tooltip sees. (That fact was measured as a hazard --
//  root.itemConfig does NOT merge them -- and this is the same mechanism used
//  deliberately rather than tripped over.)
//
//  The strings below are therefore FALLBACKS ONLY. Seeing either of them in
//  game means the row builder did not write its parameters, which is worth
//  saying rather than showing a blank box.
//
//  TWO PARAMETERS DO NOT REACH THE TOOLTIP DIRECTLY AND GO THROUGH THE BUILD
//  SCRIPT INSTEAD -- `helpIcon` and `helpSubtitle`. Parameter merging can only
//  overwrite fields the item already has; `config.tooltipFields` is how a
//  descriptor addresses a tooltip WIDGET, and only a build script can write it.
//  See petports_buildhelptooltip.lua.
//
//  ---- THE TOOLTIP LAYOUT IS OURS ----------------------------------------
//
//  THE DEFAULT WAS TRIED FIRST AND IT IS THE WRONG SHAPE. It is a tall object
//  tooltip with a rarity line, a price line and a money icon, all of which are
//  furniture on a help mark, and its icon frame scaled the art. Every other
//  item this mod ships still takes the default; this one does not, because it
//  is the only item that is a piece of interface.
//
//  `tooltipKind` NAMES A FILE. The engine resolves it into
//  /interface/tooltips/<kind>.tooltip -- so this string and that filename are
//  one interface, and a mismatch is a missing asset rather than a fallback.
//  What is in the file, and which widget names the engine will fill, is
//  documented there.
//
//  COLOUR CODES RENDER IN AN ITEM DESCRIPTION -- confirmed in game, not
//  assumed. Six of the tip bodies carry `^green;`, so this mattered.
//
//  ---- MEASURED: THE SLOT FITS ART TO ITSELF -----------------------------
//
//  OBSERVED IN GAME 2026-09-08. ItemSlotWidget does NOT draw an inventory icon
//  at natural size; it fits the art to the slot the way the inventory does, so
//  the 9px mark on its 16px canvas came out scaled to 16 and soft. That is
//  fact.tooling.imageregion holding for this widget too, which was the open
//  question this build existed to answer.
//
//  THE TOOLTIP'S COPY OF THE ICON IS UNAFFECTED, because a tooltip layout
//  states minSize and maxSize and the layout wins there. This is a row-slot
//  problem only, and the art is where it gets solved -- see CORNERS in
//  petports_helpicon.py for the pin, or simply ship a 16px mark.
//
//  ---- SAFETY ------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  price 0 and rarity Common keep the tooltip's own furniture as quiet as it
//  can be made without a layout file. petports_no_upcycling is stated even
//  though this can never reach a container, because the cost of stating it is
//  nothing and the cost of being wrong is a player's modules becoming treats.

{
	"itemName" : "petports_helptooltip",
	"price" : 0,

	//  THE FALLBACK RARITY, AND IT IS OVERWRITTEN ON MOST ROWS.
	//
	//  An itemslot draws its item's rarity as a border, so this is visible in
	//  the settings row whether or not anything wants it to be. A row owned by
	//  a module gets that module's rarity written over this by the build
	//  script; the rows that belong to the unit rather than to a module keep
	//  Common, which is the quietest frame available.
	//
	//  THE TOOLTIP NEVER SHOWS IT. petports_help.tooltip declares no
	//  rarityLabel -- see its note on omission being the lever.
	"rarity" : "Common",

	//  SHARED WITH THE MODULES so the categories.config.patch label and the
	//  petports_filtergroups.config subgroup that already exist cover it. A new
	//  category would need both adding or this sorts to Unsorted.
	"category" : "petports_module",

	"inventoryIcon" : "petports_helptooltip.png",

	//  SEE THE HEADER. The file is /interface/tooltips/petports_help.tooltip
	//  and this string is what points at it.
	"tooltipKind" : "petports_help",

	//  THE SECOND HALF OF THE TOOLTIP, AND IT IS WHAT KEEPS THE ROW'S QUESTION
	//  MARK. The layout says where things go; the builder says WHAT goes in
	//  objectImage and the subtitle, per row, from descriptor parameters --
	//  without touching inventoryIcon, which the row's own slot draws. Its
	//  header has the reasoning and the vanilla precedents. It also writes
	//  `rarity`, which is an item field rather than a tooltip one and exists
	//  purely for the border the row's slot draws.
	"builder" : "/items/buildscripts/petports_buildhelptooltip.lua",

	"description" : "^red;This help text failed to load.^reset;",
	"shortdescription" : "Help",

	"itemTags" : [ "petports", "petports_no_upcycling" ],

	//  MAXSTACK 1, AND THE SLOT NEVER SHOWS A COUNT BECAUSE OF IT.
	//  fact.pane.itemslotbutton: an itemslot DOES draw a stack number and
	//  hides a count of one, exactly as an inventory slot does. A count beside
	//  a question mark would be nonsense, so the descriptor is always count 1
	//  and this backs that up.
	"maxStack" : 1
}
